    "case_summary": "A 35-year-old woman presented 11 weeks postpartum with right breast erythema and pain. She had experienced three prior episodes of similar symptoms treated at outside hospitals, with the most recent occurring at 9 weeks postpartum. Medical history includes depression and previous treatment with cabergoline until 2016. During her first trimester of pregnancy, hormone levels were normal. Postpartum, she continued to express an excessive amount of milk despite attempts to wean. No recurrent headaches or visual changes were reported. Her menstrual cycle had not resumed, and medications included multivitamins.\n\nA 5 cm × 8 cm area of erythema and induration on the medial aspect of the right breast. No fluctuance was present, but a milky, nonpurulent discharge was easily expressed from both breasts.\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte count: 19.1 × 10⁹/L (normal: 4.5–11.0 × 10⁹/L)- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 33 mm/h (normal: 0–15 mm/h)- C-reactive protein: 11.8 mg/L (normal: <1 mg/L)- Thyroid-stimulating hormone: 1.429 mIU/L (normal: 0.55–4.78 mIU/L)- Prolactin: 72.3 ng/mL (normal: 4.8–23.3 ng/mL)- Anaerobic culture: Positive for Streptococcus agalactiae, susceptible to penicillinImaging Studies:- MRI Findings:  Title: Pituitary MRI  Description: Slightly heterogeneous appearance of the pituitary gland without evidence of discrete mass or lesion. No suprasellar mass or optic chiasm compression. Small areas of increased signal in the left frontal and right periarterial white matter.- Ultrasound Findings:  Title: Breast Ultrasound  Description: No abscess or focal lesion in the right breast.",
